 40 50  years it's translated into French that  it's released in American editions she  influences a lot of women writers  there's quite a few men who are reading  her as well  you know she's she's writing stuff  that's that's in the first caliber when  it comes to social theory and ethics and  then because William guy  when publishes his memoirs about her  it's going to erode her reputation in  part because this is something that I  didn't mention in the biography section  she did live an unconventional life in  many other respects she had a child out  of wedlock  she was the product of a love affair  with this American adventurer who kind  of throws her over there's actually love  letters that she wrote to him that are  they're extremely interesting and are  sort of exemplifying some of the things  that she's talking about in this work  when it comes to love passion marriage  desire virtue all these these sorts of  things that fit in she also had some  some rather unconventional affairs with  other people she had a few suicide  attempts and so all of these sorts of  things when they came out they you know  they're coming out in a time when  reputation and sort of being a you know  that the right kind of person was it was  considered very important for authors  and so a lot of people will then say  well we're not gonna read this stuff  because she's clearly no good was too  bad because they deprived themselves of  a great experience of reading a  wonderful classic work that that will  probably be read you know thousands of  years from now later on as feminism  becomes a real going concern in the 20th  century there is a revival of attention  to her work and people start to  understand the real importance of it and  she's now today considered one of the  most for the most important early  feminists  in some respects I think that that's a  bit too narrow because the what she's  advocating is not just virtue for women  or equality for women she's actually  making an argument a very interesting  argument about the need for both women  and men to cultivate the same kind of  virtue so that they can actually have a  relationship which is one of equality  that provides us with a good lead-in for  talking about what some of the key  themes are so you know we just mentioned  equality so you know equality versus  inequality this is one of the key themes  that she's trying to work out there  she's thinking not only about the  existence of both of those  but what creates them and what  perpetuates them particularly in  relation to women and how they are  maintained in a position of inequality  even in societies that seem to be moving  towards more and more equality she also  talks quite a bit about the nature of  civilization and in her view were not  yet fully civilized we won't be entirely  civilized civilization will be held back  until until both men and women developed  their intellectual faculties develop  their virtues and exist in a station of  equality with each other in which they  can actually have mutual respect and  relationships that are not exploitative  until we have that she thinks that we're  always going to be in a state of semi  barbarism we may think that we're  civilized but we're actually still  living out a kind of you know primitive  lifestyle that has managed to bring  itself forward you know from century to  century to century and consolidate  itself in distinctions of class  distinctions power distinctions wealth  all these sorts of things another key  theme that you're gonna see her talk  about an awful lot is this notion of  virtue so a virtue is a good character  trait and she thinks that these are what  actually human nature is supposed to be  about that we are we're only realizing  our human nature when we cultivate the  the virtues to virtues that she talks  about in particular that I think I  should single out here  our virtues that are often talked about  as feminine or women leave virtues and  she wants to say that if we understand  them in their proper sense they're there  for both men and women and whatever is  just for women in them isn't really a  virtue so she talks about gentleness and  she talks about modesty another key  theme that she's she's coming back to  over and over again is intellectual  development we we have the capacity as  human beings to develop our intellect to  develop our imagination to use our  Faculty of reason and to eventually  expand our mind to make it better and  better and better often times we don't  do that she believes and unfortunately  the kind of education that women are  getting tends to do it less than for men  this is a good point to talk about  another key theme which is education  Wollstonecraft's thinks that education  takes place outside of the schoolroom in  society in our culture in the family in  the workplace and our professions in the  ways in which our ideas are associated  the ways we live on our lifestyle the  lessons and conclusions that we draw  from them that's all education and  education can be done well or it can be  done very poorly  she thinks that most women are actually  miss educated in the society that she's  writing to another key idea is that of  Independence an education is supposed to  make us independent and independence is  one of the key components in a full  human life as a matter of fact she  thinks that we can't entirely cultivate  the virtues unless we do so from a  position where we get to decide for  ourselves where we're not just subject  to another person and so you know the  opposite of Independence would be  dependency and she's very much concerned  with the ways in which women  are kept in conditions of dependency the  ways in which they're educated towards  dependency another key idea that she  talks about that's related to this is  the emphasis on on beauty and pleasing  men as what is most important for women  so in place of you know real virtues in  place of intellectual development women  are steered towards what she calls  bodily accomplishments which are often  detrimental to their health detrimental  to their development as as human beings  and which don't actually get them what  they would want from their their mates  or spouses anyway which is respect and a  genuine kind of love she also does talk  and this is another key theme about how  we want to understand the passion of  love and by that she means you know  everything from straight-up sexual  desire to our you know sort of romantic  infatuation feelings and everything in  between  she tends to downplay this she thinks  that it's not that important in  relationships and that leads us to our  last key theme of this which is that she  thinks that the proper relationship  between men and women if women aren't  just going to be independent is in a  marriage that is based on mutual respect  for the other person as an intellectual  equal  as somebody who's displaying virtue that  you can share in and which develops into  a kind of intimacy and friendship so  those are the key themes things that you  should like keep a particular eye out  for as you go through this this great  work
 

